  (143,16,$q$What is the last year of the twentieth century that is divisible by 37?$q$,1,array['Set 3']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S3-4A'),
  (143,17,$q$In a Tribonacci sequence each number after the third is the sum of the three before it (5, 6, 7, 18, 31 …). Another one begins P, Q, 86, 158, 291. What is P?$q$,3,array['Set 3']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S3-4B'),
  (143,18,$q$Kim got 3 votes for every 2 that Amy got, and no one else ran. If 8 of Kim's voters had switched to Amy, Kim would have had only 1 vote for every 2 of Amy's. How many people voted?$q$,2,array['Set 3']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S3-4C'),
  (143,19,$q$Alma's stones each weigh a whole number of grams. Choosing the right stones she can weigh every amount from 1 g to 31 g. What is the fewest stones she can have?$q$,3,array['Set 3']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S3-4D'),
  (143,20,$q$Three circular streets cross at points A, B, C, D, E and F (see figure). How many different paths go from A to F if no point is visited more than once?$q$,3,array['Set 3']::text[],'apsmo',$q$<svg viewBox="0 0 220 200" width="220" height="200" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="13"><circle cx="110" cy="71" r="52" fill="none" stroke="#1f2130" stroke-width="1.8"/><circle cx="80" cy="123" r="52" fill="none" stroke="#1f2130" stroke-width="1.8"/><circle cx="140" cy="123" r="52" fill="none" stroke="#1f2130" stroke-width="1.8"/><circle cx="110.0" cy="80.5" r="4" fill="#5b4fcf"/><text x="110.0" y="72.52647883680939" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">A</text><circle cx="88.2" cy="118.2" r="4" fill="#5b4fcf"/><text x="76.22024636475281" y="122.21908863571953" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">D</text><circle cx="131.8" cy="118.2" r="4" fill="#5b4fcf"/><text x="143.77975363524718" y="122.21908863571953" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">E</text><circle cx="110.0" cy="165.5" r="4" fill="#5b4fcf"/><text x="110.0" y="183.4735211631906" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">F</text><circle cx="58.2" cy="75.8" r="4" fill="#5b4fcf"/><text x="46.22024636475281" y="69.78091136428047" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">B</text><circle cx="161.8" cy="75.8" r="4" fill="#5b4fcf"/><text x="173.77975363524718" y="69.78091136428047" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">C</text></svg>$q$,'S3-4E'),
  (143,21,$q$This morning Allen had 2 more action figures than Barbara, and Barbara had 8 more than Charlie. Allen gave some to Charlie, and now Allen and Charlie have the same number. Barbara now has N more than Charlie. What is N?$q$,2,array['Set 3']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S3-5A'),
  (143,22,$q$Frank read nine consecutive pages; the page numbers add to 378. What was the middle page?$q$,2,array['Set 3']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S3-5B'),
  (143,23,$q$Tanya starts at 2 and keeps adding 3. Joe starts at N (not 2) and keeps adding D (not 3). His 2nd number is 9 and his 5th is 21. What is his 1000th number?$q$,3,array['Set 3']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S3-5C'),
  (143,24,$q$In the multiplication AB × CD the first partial product has three digits, the second ends in 0, and the answer is DDD. Different letters are different non-zero digits; blanks can be any digit. What are both possible values of DDD?$q$,2,array['Set 3']::text[],'apsmo',$q$<svg viewBox="0 0 180 164" width="180" height="164" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="Georgia,serif" font-size="13"><text x="150" y="30" text-anchor="end" font-size="20" font-style="italic">A B</text><text x="150" y="58" text-anchor="end" font-size="20" font-style="italic">× C D</text><line x1="70" y1="66" x2="155" y2="66" stroke="#1f2130" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="150" y="92" text-anchor="end" font-size="20" >_ _ _</text><text x="150" y="118" text-anchor="end" font-size="20" >_ _ 0</text><line x1="70" y1="126" x2="155" y2="126" stroke="#1f2130" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="150" y="152" text-anchor="end" font-size="20" font-style="italic">D D D</text></svg>$q$,'S3-5D'),
  (143,25,$q$Candy and Sandy compete in several events (nobody else). Each event the winner gets W points and the loser L (a smaller counting number); no ties. Candy wins overall 22 to 13 and Sandy wins exactly one event. What is W?$q$,3,array['Set 3']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S3-5E'),
  (144,1,$q$How many counting numbers between 10 and 200 have digits adding to 6, if zero is not used as a digit?$q$,1,array['Set 4']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S4-1A'),
  (144,2,$q$A rectangle has perimeter 26 units and whole-number sides. What is the largest possible area?$q$,2,array['Set 4']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S4-1B'),
  (144,3,$q$Bryan buys lollies at 4 for 50c and sells them at 3 for 50c. How many must he sell to make $5.00 profit?$q$,2,array['Set 4']::text[],'apsmo',null,'S4-1C'),
